# PowerShell coverage exclusions for the xp-gate pre-commit hook.
#
# One glob per line. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# Matched source files are removed from Pester's -CodeCoverage path list.
#
# Current entries (2026-10-09): the adapter's coverage denominator is
# `find . -name '*.ps1'` over the WHOLE repo, so any non-product script on
# disk dilutes the ratio. The project baseline (84.08%, 2804/3335, 17 files:
# references/scripts + setup.ps1) covers product code only. The files below
# are excluded because they are NOT product code and are BY DESIGN never
# exercised by the Pester suite — this is a structural distinction, not the
# "hard to test" excuse that setup.ps1's entry once was (see below).
#
# -------------------------------
# wrc-drill/*.ps1 — admin drill harnesses (drill3/4/5, rdlab, teardown).
# They deliberately touch live system state (services, PATH, registry) and
# are exercised by the manual 23-check admin drill recorded in CHANGELOG,
# never by Pester; wiring them into unit tests would mean mocking away the
# very realness they exist to verify.
# -------------------------------
# .sprint-state/delphi-run.ps1 and .delphi-work/**/delphi-run.ps1 — Delphi
# multi-round review orchestration tooling (the .delphi-work copy is an
# archived worktree snapshot). Review process tooling, not shipped scripts.
# -------------------------------
wrc-drill/*.ps1
.sprint-state/delphi-run.ps1
.delphi-work/*
#
# The list was previously exactly one file, setup.ps1, on the grounds that
# its `Main` ended in `exit 0` / `exit 1` and therefore killed the Pester
# host when dot-sourced, making it "structurally unmeasurable". That was a
# code defect dressed up as a structural limit: ADR-001 moved the exit code
# to `[ref]$ExitCode` and put `exit` only in the execution guard, so
# setup.ps1 is now instrumented like every other script (it was REMOVED from
# this list in 721cada). The entries above differ: they are not product code
# and never will be.
#
# Do NOT add files here merely because they are hard to test. If a script
# really cannot be instrumented, prove it in the commit message and fix the
# script first.
